{
 "duration": 39.31,
 "lines": [
  {
   "id": "l1",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Bankrupt, legally off the hook, and he paid his debts anyway.",
   "start": 0.35,
   "end": 4.049
  },
  {
   "id": "l2",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Pittsburgh, 1875. Henry Heinz's first food company collapsed.",
   "start": 4.269,
   "end": 10.447
  },
  {
   "id": "l3",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "It owed far more than it owned.",
   "start": 10.667,
   "end": 12.256
  },
  {
   "id": "l4",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Bankruptcy wiped the slate clean. Legally, he owed nobody a cent.",
   "start": 12.476,
   "end": 16.854
  },
  {
   "id": "l5",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "In 1876 he started again, with his brother and his cousin.",
   "start": 17.074,
   "end": 22.633
  },
  {
   "id": "l6",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "But he kept his old creditors in mind, and he paid them back.",
   "start": 22.853,
   "end": 26.376
  },
  {
   "id": "l7",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "By 1888 the company carried his name: H. J. Heinz.",
   "start": 26.596,
   "end": 32.028
  },
  {
   "id": "l8",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Your word outlives bankruptcy.",
   "start": 32.248,
   "end": 34.079
  },
  {
   "id": "l9",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "So what would you do, if you were bankrupt and legally off the hook?",
   "start": 34.299,
   "end": 38.11
  }
 ],
